    "proof_tex": "Fix an accepted \\(\\mathfrak u\\), a table \\(p\\in\\mathcal M_{\\mathcal I}^{\\circ}\\), a graph \\(D\\in\\mathfrak D\\), and an outcome cell \\(y\\).  Put \\(L=J_{\\mathcal I}:s^\\infty\\).  Normalization and all oracle-one generators vanish at \\(p\\), so\n\\[\n h(p)=0\\qquad(h\\in J_{\\mathcal I}). \\tag{64}\n\\]\nEvery cell is positive.  Every recorded denominator factor is a nonempty observed marginal, hence a nonempty sum of positive cells.  Therefore\n\\[\n B_{D',y'}(p)>0\\quad(D'\\in\\mathfrak D,\\ y'),\n \\qquad s(p)\\neq0. \\tag{65}\n\\]\nIf \\(h\\in L\\), some \\(N\\geq0\\) satisfies \\(s^Nh\\in J_{\\mathcal I}\\).  Equations (64)--(65) yield \\(s(p)^Nh(p)=0\\), and division by \\(s(p)^N\\neq0\\) gives \\(h(p)=0\\).  Thus \\(p\\) lies in the real variety of \\(L\\).\n\nThe hypothesis and canonical-basis membership give\n\\[\n \\operatorname{NF}_{\\prec,K_{\\mathcal I}}(F_{D,y})=0\n \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad F_{D,y}\\in K_{\\mathcal I}. \\tag{66}\n\\]\nBy \\(K_{\\mathcal I}=\\sqrt[\\mathbb R]{L}\\), the polynomial in (66) vanishes on the real variety of \\(L\\), hence \\(F_{D,y}(p)=0\\).  Using \\(\\mathrm{def{:}cross\\text{-}signature}\\) and the positive denominators in (65),\n\\[\n 0=F_{D,y}(p)\n =B_{D,y}(p)B_{D_0,y}(p)\n \\{T_{D,y}(p)-T_{D_0,y}(p)\\}, \\tag{67}\n\\]\nso division proves \\(T_{D,y}(p)=T_{D_0,y}(p)\\).  Since \\(p,D,y\\) were arbitrary, all asserted equalities hold throughout the exact-oracle model.\n\nBy \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}common\\text{-}law\\text{-}redundancy}\\), \\(p\\) is Markov to \\(D\\) and \\(D_0\\).  Applying \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}dag\\text{-}intervention\\text{-}functional}\\) gives\n\\[\n T_{D,y}(p)=P_x^D(Y=y),\n \\qquad T_{D_0,y}(p)=P_x^{D_0}(Y=y). \\tag{68}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\tau(p)=(T_{D_0,y}(p))_y\\) is proved to equal the common causal law; it is not declared equal by definition.  Finally, \\(\\mathrm{lem{:}certificate\\text{-}size}\\) supplies a finite real-Nullstellensatz and saturation identity for every zero normal form in (66), with deterministic verification polynomial in its total expanded length.  This proves the certificate assertion and the lemma.",
